#![TODO]

// Pending bug: rebinding a field of a struct returned by a function in
// an `export fn` body ICEs the WIR pipeline with
// "WIR pipeline generated invalid core Wasm module: type mismatch:
//  expected (ref $type), found (ref (exact $type))".
//
// Minimum trigger (every clause is required — dropping any one makes the
// ICE go away):
//
//   1. Caller is `export fn` (plain `fn` does not trigger).
//   2. Caller binds the returned struct to a NAMED local, then reads a
//      field. Inlining (`build(...).a`) does not trigger.
//   3. The struct has at least TWO Array-typed fields. With one Array
//      field plus an i32 (or two String fields with no Array), the ICE
//      goes away — the trigger is specific to multiple GC-array fields
//      sharing the same struct.
//   4. The function body uses a divergent expression — `let _ = if cond
//      { value } else { return EarlyReturn; };` — that returns the same
//      struct shape from the early-return branch as from the trailing
//      explicit `return`. A statement-form `if cond { ... }; return ...`
//      that does not bind a value does not trigger.
//
// Surfaced while wiring Stage B of the ANTLR4 descriptor extractor at
// `package-gale/scripts/extract_antlr4_descriptors.wado`, where the
// `load_triage_maps` function (returning a TriageMaps struct with
// stage_a/stage_b TriageMap fields, each holding `Array<TriageEntry>`)
// used `let text = match content { Some(t) => t, None => return
// TriageMaps { stage_a, stage_b } }` and the export `run()` did
// `let triage_maps = load_triage_maps(...); let triage_map =
// triage_maps.stage_a;`.
//
// The expected (correct) behaviour is: the program compiles and runs to
// completion with no output.
//
// The bug is opt-level-dependent — at -O0 the module compiles cleanly,
// but at -O1 and above the WIR pipeline trips validation. The fixture
// is scoped via `only_opt: ["O1", "O2", "O3", "Os"]` so the failing
// levels run; at those levels the module's `#![TODO]` tag tells the e2e
// harness to `catch_unwind` the panic and report the fixture as
// expected failure (otherwise CI would break on every run). When the
// underlying bug is fixed, drop both the `#![TODO]` and the `only_opt`
// — the harness will then enforce that the fixture compiles cleanly at
// every level and emits the empty stdout asserted below.

use { Stdout } from "core:cli";

struct Outer {
    a: Array<i32>,
    b: Array<i32>,
}

fn build(flag: bool) -> Outer {
    let a: Array<i32> = [];
    let b: Array<i32> = [];
    let _x: i32 = if flag { 1 } else { return Outer { a, b }; };
    return Outer { a, b };
}

export fn run() with Stdout {
    let outer = build(true);
    let _x = outer.a;
}

__DATA__
{"stdout": "", "only_opt": ["O1", "O2", "O3", "Os"]}
